{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["A network operator can perturb the shared-ancestor hypothesis in software by coordinated patching, whereas natural-world causal graphs are often not intervenable and independence must be inferred from observational covariation.","Administrative decentralisation is a proxy for causal disjointness, not a guarantee: two independent operators can independently choose the same single upstream library, making the graph single-ancestor despite administrative diversity.","Causal perturbation tests can identify latent shared causes, but they cannot prove the absence of all remaining latent shared causes; one can reduce but not eliminate uncertainty about undiscovered common ancestors."],"overturn_conditions":["Demonstrate two nodes that pass rate-decorrelation and intervention tests yet still share an undetected common ancestor that activates only on an unprobed regime (proving the intervention frame was incomplete).","Demonstrate that the rare-tail contribution of shared-ancestor failures is negligible in practice (shared ancestors are real but do not dominate correlated risk), weakening the practical importance of the distinction.","Demonstrate that a strictly-more-redundant single service has such high internal redundancy that its residual shared-ancestor tail is smaller than the decentralised network's aggregate independent-failure tail, reversing the resilience ordering."],"shared_structure":["Independence is structurally a disjoint-path property of a causal graph, not merely a low observed correlation coefficient.","Latent common-mode dependencies act as hidden common ancestors: their rare activation correlates failures that looked independent under the sampled regime.","Disjoint-path graphs fail under distributed exogenous shocks; single-ancestor graphs fail under targeted ancestor shocks; these are different regimes even when rates look similar in-baseline."],"source_memory_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"transfer_claims":["The distinguishing evidence is not passive covariation alone but intervention: a true independence claim should be sensitive to whether remote node hazards move under local perturbations.","A decentralised network is more resilient than one large redundant service only where its implementation diversity and upstream diversity make the causal graph genuinely more disjoint, not merely where it is administratively fragmented.","Evidence that distinguishes independent from merely-uncorrelated failures must include adversarial common-cause probes that sample the rare tail where shared ancestors activate."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Failure modes that merely appear uncorrelated until a shared cause is found are produced by latent common-mode dependencies (e.g., shared software dependencies, synchronised operational practices, correlated upstream supply chains) that travel the same low-frequency causal pathway, whereas truly independent failure modes have causally disjoint path sets; the distinguishing evidence is observing whether interventions targeting the local implementation of one node alter the hazard rate of geographically and administratively remote nodes, which cannot occur under independence but is the signature of a shared latent causal path.","counterconditions":["If intervention on one node's implementation (patching its specific software, changing its specific operator) measurably lowers the hazard of remote nodes, independence is falsified and a shared latent cause is identified.","If the cross-node failure hazard does not change when an independent operator rotates away from a common upstream supplier, but does change when a shared software dependency is patched everywhere, the shared cause is localised to software, not to independence generally.","If two nodes pass rate-decorrelation tests under normal conditions but fail after an adversarial common-cause stress test reveals identical failure semantics, the apparent independence was an artefact of an unsampled regime, not true causal disjointness."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Independence is a property of the causal graph, not the failure rate vector: when two nodes do not share a directed path from any common ancestor, interventions on one cannot change the other's hazard. In many decentralised networks the causal graph is more sparse and disjoint (many independently-versioned implementations, many independent operators), which mechanically reduces the set of common ancestors. But an apparent-decentralisation layer can still sit on top of a single shared ancestor (one shared cloud region, one shared library, one shared upstream supplier, one shared default config); in that case the graph is only disjoint below the shared node and failure rate observations can look independent until the shared ancestor's rare failure dominates the tail. Distinguishing evidence therefore requires perturbing the shared ancestor or observing independent perturbations at the leaves, which separates true causal-disjointness from mere rate-decorrelation.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55}],"simulation_proposals":[]}